{
  "requirement": "Habeas's bundled agent skills MUST jointly give the direction that the requirements on them specify. A skill consists of its `SKILL.md` and the files under its `references/` directory. Each topic MUST be stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations in `requirements.cli.skill_structure`:**\n1. The bundled skills jointly give the direction that the requirements on them specify. The per-skill home requirements (`requirements.cli.skill_structure.habeas`, `.requirements`, `.evidence`, `.bootstrap`, `.refactor`) and the content requirements refine this. Each has its own tests and review, so I did not reassess them here.\n2. A skill is its `SKILL.md` plus the files under its `references/` directory.\n3. Each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, its home.\n\n**Mapping to `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_are_five_skill_directories_each_home_to_its_topics]`:**\n- **Skill definition (2).**\n  - For every skill in `SKILLS`, the test lists the files on disk (`SKILL.md` plus each entry under `references/`). It asserts they equal the files `skill_files(name)` lists.\n  - Those are the files `bundled_skill` reads, so the text the tests judge is exactly the skill as defined.\n  - It asserts that the `assets/skills` directory contains exactly the five expected skill directories.\n  - It asserts that each `SKILL.md` front matter carries the skill's name.\n  - It asserts that the Symposium manifest publishes `assets/skills`.\n- **Exactly one home (3).** For each skill, `assert_home` runs on `home_markers(home)`.\n  - It asserts that the home's text contains each marker phrase, through `assert_phrases` on `bundled_skill`, which joins `SKILL.md` and the references.\n  - It asserts that none of the other four skills contains that marker.\n  - Together these check both \"stated in the home\" and \"not restated elsewhere\".\n  - The markers cover the topics the refining requirements assign to each home:\n    - `habeas`: the routing table, selections, directive and reference syntax, distributed sites and gotchas.\n    - `habeas-requirements`: drafting, review criteria and the root definition.\n    - `habeas-evidence`: the authoring workflow, fixing findings, outcomes and FIXME judgments, citation and site reviews, disagreeing assessments and the commit message.\n    - `habeas-bootstrap-requirements`: deriving an initial requirement set.\n    - `habeas-refactor`: moving and renaming.\n- **Delegated obligations (1).** The joint direction is delegated to the refining requirements and their own tests.\n\n**Limits considered and not treated as gaps.** The \"in full\" and \"no restatement\" checks work through representative marker phrases and cannot detect every paraphrase. The requirement states no finer, checkable form, and it is a content-level property. Each topic named by the home requirements has markers, and the helper asserts both presence in the home and absence elsewhere. The assertions would catch a marker phrase being moved, duplicated or dropped. They would also catch an extra or missing skill directory or reference file. I found no clause of the subject left without a test.",
  "references": "5a5c704ea25b3efceb4b7c4b3d3f998ccd6490c969be6f0fcaaf7011ddc4cf17",
